# Cloudflare Pages response headers for cartogenesis.com.
#
# Read this before adding a rule: when two rules match the same URL and both set the same
# header, Pages *joins the values with a comma* rather than letting the later one win. Two
# rules that both set Cache-Control would therefore emit
# "public, max-age=31536000, immutable, no-cache", which is nonsense. So the rules below are
# arranged to be mutually exclusive on Cache-Control, and the site-wide rule sets nothing but
# the sniffing header.
#
# Not here on purpose:
#   Content-Type   Pages already serves .wasm as application/wasm, which is what the
#                  streaming compiler insists on. Stating it again could only get it wrong.
#   COOP / COEP    Only needed for SharedArrayBuffer. Nothing here uses threads.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# The two .wasm files carry content hashes in their names, so a given URL is that build's
# bytes forever and can be cached for a year. cartogenesis.js does not — but the shell stamps
# ?v=<commit> into its URL on every deploy, and the shell itself is never cached (below), so a
# visitor always reads a current shell and therefore always asks for a current loader. An old
# cached shell can only ask for the old loader, which is still there under its old query.
/app/*.wasm
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/app/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The two pages must never be stale: the shell is what carries the loader stamp, so a cached
# copy of it is exactly the failure the stamp exists to prevent.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/app/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The apt repository, in two halves that want opposite caching and are kept on separate rules
# so neither joins a Cache-Control onto the other (see the note at the top).
#
# A package under pool/ carries its version in its name, so that URL is those bytes for ever.
# The indexes under dists/ are the opposite: `apt update` fetches them to find out what is new,
# and a cached Release file is a reader who cannot see the release that was just published. The
# signature is checked against the index, so a stale pair of them is quiet rather than loud.
/apt/pool/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/apt/dists/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
